(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-mi-antianginals-quiz"]={"title":"Antianginal Drugs Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which risk factor for coronary heart disease can a patient change?","choices":["Tobacco use","Age older than 45 in men","Age older than 55 in women","Family history of premature events"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tobacco use, a sedentary lifestyle, diabetes, overweight, hypertension and dyslipidemia are modifiable risk factors that therapy targets. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Patients starting a calcium channel blocker are counseled about dizziness and which other problem?","choices":["Nightmares","Hyperglycemia","Constipation","Sexual dysfunction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dizziness and constipation are the counseling points for calcium channel blockers. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"In the traditional comorbidity-based approach, which class is first line for angina in a patient with diabetes who has no other reason to need a beta blocker?","choices":["Beta blockers","Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The traditional comorbidity table gives a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker as first line in diabetes, with a long-acting nitrate or a cardioselective beta blocker as alternatives. Current practice is more flexible: a cardioselective beta blocker remains a sound first choice. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which action of calcium channel blockers improves myocardial oxygen supply?","choices":["Stabilizing atherosclerotic plaque","Blocking platelet aggregation","Relief of coronary vasospasm","Reducing ventricular volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Calcium channel blockers relieve vasospasm and mildly dilate areas of fixed stenosis, raising supply, and they also lower demand. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient taking verapamil is started on a statin. Which statin's level rises most?","choices":["Simvastatin","Rosuvastatin","Pravastatin","Pitavastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Simvastatin is a cytochrome P450 3A4 substrate, and verapamil inhibits that enzyme, so simvastatin levels climb (statin metabolism is covered with the lipid-lowering drugs). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which blood pressure reading contraindicates starting a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker?","choices":["Systolic pressure above 140 mmHg","Diastolic pressure above 90 mmHg","Systolic pressure below 100 mmHg","Systolic pressure below 130 mmHg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both classes lower blood pressure, so a systolic pressure below 100 mmHg contraindicates them. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which valve lesion contraindicates nitrates?","choices":["Mitral valve prolapse","Aortic valve stenosis","Aortic regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aortic valve stenosis is a nitrate contraindication, alongside obstructive cardiomyopathy and phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor use. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Angina persists despite two antianginal drugs. What is the usual next step?","choices":["Lifestyle counseling alone","Further workup, such as angiography","Adding an antiplatelet for symptoms","Adding a third drug without reassessment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If a third agent seems needed, the patient probably needs further workup such as angiography rather than another drug. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which drug is taken every day to prevent anginal attacks rather than to relieve an acute one?","choices":["Metoprolol","Sublingual nitroglycerin","Nitroglycerin lingual spray","Aspirin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers, calcium channel blockers and long-acting nitrates are taken to prevent attacks; they are not rescue drugs. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How are long-acting nitrates usually used?","choices":["As first-line monotherapy","As add-on therapy","Only for acute attacks","Only after an infarction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They are usually adjunctive, added when a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker is not enough; monotherapy is not recommended. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which condition favors choosing a calcium channel blocker over a beta blocker?","choices":["Prior myocardial infarction","Hypertension with angina","Vasospastic angina","Anxiety with angina"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Calcium channel blockers relieve vasospasm, while beta blockers may worsen vasospastic angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A patient with peripheral vascular disease starts a beta blocker. Which symptom may worsen?","choices":["Ankle swelling","Facial flushing","Constipation","Leg pain with walking"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Claudication is listed as a possible beta blocker adverse reaction, which is why peripheral vascular disease is a precaution. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What should patients starting a nitrate be warned about?","choices":["Constipation after each dose","Dizziness when standing up","Slow heart rate at rest","Persistent dry cough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates cause orthostatic hypotension, so patients are warned about light-headedness on standing. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the loss of nitrate effect with continuous use called?","choices":["Anaphylaxis","Reflex tachycardia","Rebound angina","Tachyphylaxis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tachyphylaxis is rapid tolerance to a nitrate's effect, handled with a nitrate-free interval. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A patient taking a beta blocker feels well and wants to stop it. What should he be told?","choices":["Avoid stopping it abruptly","Stop it once symptoms resolve","Take it only when chest pain starts","Skip it on days without exertion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid discontinuation of a beta blocker must be avoided, so the patient should not stop it on his own. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which antianginal type is best avoided in a patient with angina and asthma?","choices":["Non-selective beta blockers","Beta-1 selective beta blockers","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In asthma, non-selective beta blockers are avoided; cardioselective beta blockers and calcium channel blockers remain options. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A patient started on a beta blocker for angina cannot tolerate it because of nightmares. Which drug is the usual substitute?","choices":["Diltiazem","Isosorbide mononitrate","Sublingual nitroglycerin","Lisinopril"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker is the usual substitute when a beta blocker is not tolerated, since it also lowers heart rate and contractility. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Traditionally, when should a patient replace sublingual nitroglycerin tablets after opening the bottle?","choices":["Every 1 to 2 weeks","Every 12 to 18 months","Every 3 to 6 months","Only when the bottle is empty"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The traditional counseling is to replace opened tablets every 3 to 6 months, so that a fresh supply is on hand in an emergency. Current labeling instead ties expiry to the printed date when the tablets stay in the tightly closed original glass bottle. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which benefit do antianginal drugs provide?","choices":["Regression of coronary plaque","Removal of coronary stenosis","Prevention of clot formation","Better exercise capacity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Antianginals improve exercise capacity, reduce exercise-induced ST-segment changes and lower the frequency of symptoms. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker typically raises heart rate?","choices":["Nifedipine","Verapamil","Diltiazem","Amlodipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nifedipine is a strong dihydropyridine vasodilator, and the drop in pressure prompts a reflex rise in heart rate. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 34-year-old nonsmoker has chest pain that wakes her at 4 a.m., with ST-segment elevation that resolves. Which antianginal class is best avoided?","choices":["Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Beta blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is vasospastic (variant) angina; beta blockers may worsen symptoms, while calcium channel blockers and nitrates reduce them. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of nitrates is a compensatory response to vasodilation?","choices":["Bradycardia","Reflex tachycardia","Heart block","Hyperglycemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Falling pressure from vasodilation prompts a reflex rise in heart rate; it is listed with headache, flushing and postural hypotension. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which drug class must not be combined with nitrates?","choices":["Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sildenafil, tadalafil and vardenafil with a nitrate can cause hypotension, myocardial infarction or stroke. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Long-acting nitrates become initial therapy when which drugs cannot be used?","choices":["Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers","Beta blockers alone","Calcium channel blockers alone","Aspirin and clopidogrel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A long-acting nitrate is initial therapy only when both beta blockers and calcium channel blockers are contraindicated or not tolerated. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A patient's chest pain is not relieved 5 minutes after the first nitroglycerin dose. What should he do?","choices":["Wait 30 minutes before repeating","Call emergency services","Switch to a long-acting nitrate","Rest and see if the pain settles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. No relief after the first dose means he should call emergency services, while repeat doses continue until help arrives. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is beta-1 selective?","choices":["Propranolol","Metoprolol","Nadolol","Carvedilol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metoprolol and atenolol are beta-1 selective beta blockers. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which drug is recommended for patients allergic to aspirin?","choices":["Heparin","Enoxaparin","Alteplase","Clopidogrel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clopidogrel is as efficacious as aspirin in secondary prevention and is recommended when aspirin cannot be used because of allergy. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Why do verapamil and diltiazem call for caution with a beta blocker?","choices":["Additive reflex tachycardia","Reduced absorption of the beta blocker","Additive bradycardia and conduction delay","Loss of blood pressure lowering effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both lower heart rate and slow atrioventricular conduction, so combining them with a beta blocker adds to these effects. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which enzyme's interactions call for caution with calcium channel blockers?","choices":["Cytochrome P450 2D6","Cytochrome P450 2C9","Cytochrome P450 3A4","Cytochrome P450 1A2"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Calcium channel blockers are subject to cytochrome P450 3A4 interactions, so drugs that inhibit or induce it change their levels. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How do beta blockers directly change myocardial oxygen supply?","choices":["They dilate the coronary arteries","They relieve coronary vasospasm","They have no effect on supply","They dilate areas of fixed stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers act on the demand side, lowering heart rate and contractility; they do not dilate the coronary arteries, so other antianginals are needed to raise supply. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which drug is vasculoprotective rather than antianginal?","choices":["Metoprolol","Amlodipine","Isosorbide mononitrate","Aspirin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin, statins and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors are the vasculoprotective group; beta blockers, calcium channel blockers and nitrates are the antianginals. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which heart condition is a contraindication to beta blockers?","choices":["Anxiety with angina","Acute decompensated heart failure","Supraventricular arrhythmia","Prior myocardial infarction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers can worsen an acutely decompensated heart; they are contraindicated there, along with bradycardia, low pressure and heart block. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"}]};
